\title{An Uncountable Discrete Space Is Not Second-Countable}
\taxon{Example}
\p{
    Let #{X} be an uncountable set with the [discrete topology](def-005W). For
    every #{x\in X}, the one-element collection #{\{\{x\}\}} is a local basis
    at #{x}; hence #{X} is first-countable.
}
\p{
    On the other hand, every basis #{\mathcal{B}} for #{X} must contain every
    singleton. Indeed, since #{\{x\}} is open, the basis property requires
    some #{B\in\mathcal{B}} with
    ##{
        x\in B\subseteq\{x\},
    }
    and therefore #{B=\{x\}}. Thus #{\mathcal{B}} is uncountable, so #{X} is
    not second-countable. This also shows why the two countability axioms are
    genuinely different.
}
